-- title:
--   Lemma 4, p.25 — the two-sided regulator under $q^*$ (corrected: existence for almost every Brownian path, not every path)
-- statement:
--   Let $(f,B,\gamma)$ solve (13) with $b_j\mu_j/\lambda_j>c_k\mu_k$, let $0<b\le B$, and let $\beta$ be the drift of $q^*$.
--
--   1. For every $x\in\mathbb D$ with $x(0)\in[0,b]$, a triple $(w,\ell,u)\in\mathbb D^3$ with
--   $$w(t)=x(t)-\int_0^t\beta(w(y))\,dy+\ell(t)-u(t)\in[0,b]$$
--   and C2–C3 is unique.
--   2. Such triples are non-anticipative in $x$.
--   3. If $\beta$ is continuous on $[0,b]$, such a triple exists for every such $x$.
--   4. If $X$ is a Brownian motion with drift $0$ and variance $\Sigma$, such a triple exists for $x=X+\theta e$ for almost every path.
--
--   The lemma gives the barrier policy its meaning.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - correction: a triple exists for every x ∈ D with x(0) ∈ [0,b] → existence for every x if β is continuous on [0,b], and for almost every path of X + θe; uniqueness and non-anticipativity stay for every x; reason: the printed claim is false, and its proof patches intervals without treating a hit of a jump of β from below: n=2, μ=(1,1), λ=(1,2), b=(1,3/2), c=(3/5,3/5), S=0, any h>0, θ≤0, Σ>0, so that bjμj/λj=3/4>3/5=ckμk. For (f,B,γ) of Lemma 2 Part ii, f′ rises strictly from 0 to 3/5 on [0,B], so f′(w₀)=1/2 at one w₀∈(0,B), and β(w)=w on [0,w₀), β(w)=2w on [w₀,B]. For x(t)=w₀/2+(3/2)w₀t no triple satisfies C1–C3 with b=B: w climbs to w₀, then the slope is at most −w₀/2 at or above w₀ and at least w₀/2 below it, so w can neither leave w₀ nor stay there
--   - reading: λ ∈ ℝ^I₊, μ, s, b free, with q* from Definition 2 → s = S, the parameters of (13), q* built from a solution (f,B,γ), and 0 < b ≤ B; reason: q* depends on f and S; the proof uses that f′ is strictly increasing, which holds on [0,B] only
--   - reading: q* is "an optimal solution to (14)" (Definition 2), κ(w) chosen so that β is right continuous (OA p.26) → q* fixed: the fill of OA A.2 for w ≤ Σᵢ Sᵢ/μᵢ, otherwise κ with largest λ, then least index; reason: Definition 2 leaves ties open; this is the choice the paper names on OA pp.2 and 26
--   - generalization: Σ := Σᵢ αᵢλᵢ(1+σᵢ²)/μᵢ² (p.15) → Σ > 0 a free parameter; reason: every statement depends on Σ only through Σ > 0; the source value is one such Σ
--   - encoding: SKU set {1,…,I}, indices j, k → Fin n with n ≥ 1; bjμj/λj and ckμk as minima; reason: only the values of the minima enter the statements
--   - encoding: f ∈ C₂ with domain ℝ₊ → f : ℝ → ℝ, C² on ℝ, with (13) imposed for w ≥ 0; reason: a C² function on [0,∞) extends to ℝ, so no solution is lost
--   - generalization: 𝔽 generated by (Xᵢ) → any filtration for which X is a Brownian motion; reason: the natural filtration is one such filtration
--   - encoding: C1's integral ∫₀ᵗ (drift)(w(y)) dy and C3: ∫₀^∞ w dℓ = ∫₀^∞ (b − w) du = 0 → the drift path y ↦ β(w(y)) interval-integrable on every [0,t]; dℓ, du as Stieltjes measures of ℓ, u extended by 0 to t < 0, giving no mass to {w > 0} resp. {w < b} on [0,∞); reason: β∘w is bounded and measurable for càdlàg w with values in [0,b], so integrability excludes no triple; since w ∈ [0,b] the two vanishing integrals say exactly that these sets are null
-- source:
--   Özkan and van Houtum, Joint Inventory and Scheduling Control in a Repair Facility, SSRN 3784144 (2023), Definition 5 and Lemma 4, p.25; OA pp.25–27

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_RepairSched_Model_IsBellman
import Definitions.Def_RepairSched_Model_backorderRate
import Definitions.Def_RepairSched_Model_backorderRatio
import Definitions.Def_RepairSched_IsRegulated
import Definitions.Def_AtaBarjesteh23_Workload_DriftBM

open MeasureTheory Set

namespace RepairSched

/-- **Lemma 4: the two-sided regulator under `q*`** (corrected).
Özkan and van Houtum, *Joint Inventory and Scheduling Control in a Repair Facility*, SSRN 3784144 (2023), Definition 5 and Lemma 4, p.25, with the proof of OA A.4.2–A.4.3 (PDF pp.65–67).

Let `(f, B, γ)` satisfy (13) with `bjμj/λj > ckμk`, let `0 < b ≤ B`, and let `β` be the drift of
`q*`. Triples satisfying C1–C3 of Definition 5 are unique for every `x ∈ D` with
`x(0) ∈ [0,b]`, and they are non-anticipative with respect to `x`. They exist for every such `x`
if `β` is continuous on `[0,b]`, and they exist for almost every path of `X + θe` when `X` is a
Brownian motion with drift `0` and variance `Σ`.

**Formalization Note.** The printed lemma asserts existence for every `x ∈ D`. That is false when `β`
has a jump: see the `Differences from the source` of this item. `q*` depends on `f` and on `S`, so
`s = S` and `b ≤ B`, which is where `f′` is strictly increasing and `β` is nondecreasing. -/
theorem lemma_4 {n : ℕ} [NeZero n] (M : Model n) (S : Fin n → ℝ) (hS : ∀ i, 0 ≤ S i)
    (hEm : M.emergencyRate < M.backorderRatio) (f : ℝ → ℝ) (B γ : ℝ)
    (hBell : M.IsBellman S f B γ) (b : ℝ) (hb : 0 < b) (hbB : b ≤ B)
    {Ω : Type*} [mΩ : MeasurableSpace Ω] (𝔽 : Filtration ℝ mΩ) (P : Measure Ω)
    [IsProbabilityMeasure P] (X : ℝ → Ω → ℝ)
    (hX : AtaBarjesteh23.Workload.IsDriftBM 𝔽 P X 0 (Real.sqrt M.Sigma)) :
    (∀ x, IsCadlag x → x 0 ∈ Icc 0 b →
      ∀ w₁ l₁ u₁ w₂ l₂ u₂, IsRegulated (M.backorderRate S f) b x w₁ l₁ u₁ →
        IsRegulated (M.backorderRate S f) b x w₂ l₂ u₂ →
        EqOn w₁ w₂ (Ici 0) ∧ EqOn l₁ l₂ (Ici 0) ∧ EqOn u₁ u₂ (Ici 0)) ∧
    (∀ x₁ x₂ t, IsCadlag x₁ → IsCadlag x₂ → x₁ 0 ∈ Icc 0 b → x₂ 0 ∈ Icc 0 b → 0 ≤ t →
      EqOn x₁ x₂ (Icc 0 t) →
      ∀ w₁ l₁ u₁ w₂ l₂ u₂, IsRegulated (M.backorderRate S f) b x₁ w₁ l₁ u₁ →
        IsRegulated (M.backorderRate S f) b x₂ w₂ l₂ u₂ →
        EqOn w₁ w₂ (Icc 0 t) ∧ EqOn l₁ l₂ (Icc 0 t) ∧ EqOn u₁ u₂ (Icc 0 t)) ∧
    (ContinuousOn (M.backorderRate S f) (Icc 0 b) → ∀ x, IsCadlag x → x 0 ∈ Icc 0 b →
      ∃ w l u, IsRegulated (M.backorderRate S f) b x w l u) ∧
    ∀ᵐ ω ∂P, ∃ w l u,
      IsRegulated (M.backorderRate S f) b (fun t => X t ω + M.theta * t) w l u := by sorry

end RepairSched
